For a strong keyboard case using MJF, **PA12** is the standard workhorse, but **PA11** offers superior impact resistance, and **PAC Nylon** (often referred to as PA12-GB or glass-filled variants, or translucent-grade engineering variants depending on the specific supplier lineup) changes the rigidity profile.

# Material Comparison for Keyboard Cases

| Property | PA12 (Polyamide 12) | PA11 (Polyamide 11) | PAC / Glass-Filled Nylon |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Stiffness / Rigidity** | High (great structural stability) | Moderate (higher elongation at break) | **Very High** (reinforced) |
| **Impact Resistance** | Good | **Excellent** (more ductile, absorbs shock better) | Lower (more brittle, prone to snapping under sudden heavy stress) |
| **Dimensional Accuracy** | Excellent (very stable shrinkage rate) | Good (slightly higher warping tendencies) | Excellent |
| **Primary Use Case** | General housings, structural parts, threaded inserts | Parts requiring high ductility, living hinges, drop-resistant items | Rigid plates, thick structural frames needing zero flex |

